    "https://jobs.hr.txstate.edu/postings/58318": {
      "title": "Campus Expression & Community Standards Coordinator",
      "description": "Texas State University Applicant Portal | Campus Expression &amp; Community Standards Coordinator Skip to Main Content Job Opportunities at Texas State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Faculty and Academic Resources Human Resources Student Campus Expression & Community Standards Coordinator Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is designated as essential personnel and may require availability to work evenings, weekends, and during emergency or critical operations as needed. Posting Information Posting Information Posting Number 2026759 Posting/Functional Title Campus Expression & Community Standards Coordinator University Pay Plan Title Student Development Specialist III Location San Marcos Department Health & Wellbeing Recruitment Type Open Recruiting Job Type Full-Time Funding Source Permanent Monthly Salary $4,483-$5,666.67 Job Category Exempt Required Qualifications: Applicants must specifically address how they meet these required qualifications to meet the requirements of the position. Bachelor’s degree in higher education, student affairs, public administration, criminal justice, or a related field Experience coordinating programs, operations, or cross-functional initiatives Demonstrated ability to manage sensitive situations with sound judgment and discretion Strong collaboration and communication skills, with the ability to operate effectively in dynamic environments Ability to work evenings and weekends as required, including on-call responsibilities during high-visibility events or urgent situations Knowledge of freedom of expression/speech laws and policies, particularly as applicable to public college campuses Strong facilitation, documentation, and report-writing skills Sound judgment under pressure with a focus on de-escalation and operational consistency Experience supporting expressive activity, demonstrations, or large-scale event response Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in higher education, student affairs, criminal justice, or related field Experience conducting student conduct investigations and/or adjudication Ability to coordinate across multiple stakeholders, including law enforcement and compliance units Job Description Reporting to the Associate Vice President for Student Success and Dean of Students, the Campus Expression & Community Standards Coordinator provides primary operational coordination for the university’s expressive activity monitoring and response program, including both administrative operations and real-time campus response. The position coordinates expressive activity readiness and response protocols; event monitoring and documentation; deployment and coordination of campus responders; communication with campus partners; tracking and maintaining records; and post-event follow-up. The position works closely with the Dean of Students Office, University Police Department ( UPD ), Environmental, Health, Safety, Risk and Emergency Management ( EHSREM ), and other university partners to support consistent, coordinated, and compliant responses that protect free expression while promoting campus safety and adherence to university policy. The Campus Expression & Community Standards Coordinator also supports large-event student behavior monitoring and response, divisional compliance tracking and reporting, and Student Conduct and Community Standards. This position serves as an investigator for complex student conduct cases and provides additional investigative support as needed. Across these responsibilities, the position supports effective coordination, accurate documentation and reporting, student accountability, and institutional compliance. Job Duties Expressive Activity Program Coordination, Monitoring, and Response Coordinate the administrative and real-time operations of the university’s Expressive Activity Monitoring and Response Program, ensuring consistent implementation of university policies, procedures, and response protocols. Coordinate the Expressive Activity Response Team ( EART ), including responder recruitment and training, on-call schedules, activation and notification protocols, deployment, documentation, and after-action processes. Monitor and track expressive activities across university locations and maintain accurate records of planned and unplanned activities, university response, emerging concerns, and outcomes. Coordinate readiness planning for demonstrations, speakers, counter-demonstrations, protests, and other high-visibility expressive activities, including staffing, communication, documentation, de-escalation, and escalation protocols. Serve as a primary on-site coordinator for expressive activities, facilitating communication and coordinated response among university responders and campus partners. Provide real-time guidance regarding university expressive activity policies, freedom of expression, time, place, and manner requirements, and established campus procedures. Coordinate with UPD , EHSREM , Student Involvement and Engagement, and other campus partners to assess operational and safety considerations and determine appropriate university response. Proactively engage student organizations, advisors, event organizers, and campus partners to support awareness of and compliance with expressive activity requirements. Develop and deliver training, guidance, and resources for university personnel responsible for monitoring or responding to expressive activities. Conduct post-event reviews and prepare event summaries, after-action reports, trend analyses, and recommendations to strengthen institutional preparedness and response. Analyze expressive activity data and emerging trends to identify operational needs, potential risks, and opportunities for continuous improvement. Large-Event Student Behavior Monitoring and Response Coordinate student behavior monitoring and response for athletic events, commencements, major campus programs, and other high-attendance or high-visibility university events. Assist with developing event-specific staffing, communication, documentation, escalation, and response protocols in collaboration with appropriate campus partners. Serve in an on-site coordination role during designated events, supporting de-escalation, community standards, policy compliance, and appropriate referral of incidents. Document significant student behavior incidents and coordinate referrals to Student Conduct and Community Standards or other appropriate university offices for follow-up. Identify recurring student behavior trends and recommend education, prevention, or operational strategies to reduce future concerns. Student Conduct Investigations and Case Support Serve as an investigator for complex Student Conduct and Community Standards cases and provide additional investigative capacity when needed. Conduct neutral, thorough, and timely investigations into alleged violations of the Code of Student Conduct and other applicable university policies. Gather and analyze information, conduct interviews, review relevant records and evidence, and prepare investigative reports, findings, and case materials consistent with established procedures. Maintain appropriate investigative records and coordinate with Student Conduct and Community Standards leadership throughout the investigative process. Divisional Compliance and Risk Management Support Support the Division of Student Success compliance reporting program, including tracking reporting requirements and deadlines and coordinating the collection, verification, organization, and submission of required information. Assist divisional units in maintaining documentation and records necessary to demonstrate compliance with applicable university, system, state, and federal requirements. Serve on the Division of Student Success Risk Management Committee. Assist with identifying and assessing operational, safety, compliance, and reputational risks affecting divisional programs and activities. Translate applicable policies and requirements into operational guidance, workflows, tools, and training to support consistent implementation. Assist with compliance, risk, and trend reports for divisional leadership and monitor completion of identified corrective or mitigation actions. Emergency Preparedness and Response Support Division of Student Success responsibilities related to campus emergency preparedness and response, particularly when incidents involve students, expressive activity, or large campus events. Participate in coordinated university response efforts and assist with implementing established communication, documentation, escalation, and after-hours response protocols. Collaborate with UPD , EHSREM , the Dean of Students Office, and other campus partners during emergencies and significant incidents affecting students or the campus community. Participate in emergency preparedness exercises, after-action reviews, and improvement planning relevant to the position’s responsibilities. This position is designated as essential personnel and may require availability to work evenings, weekends, and during emergency or critical operations as needed. General Administrative and Professional Responsibilities Maintain high standards of confidentiality, professionalism, neutrality, accuracy, and documentation across all assigned responsibilities. Maintain current knowledge of university policies and applicable requirements related to expressive activity, student conduct, institutional compliance, and emergency response. Serve on committees, task forces, and divisional initiatives related to assigned responsibilities. Support assessment and continuous improvement of processes related to expressive activity, large-event response, student conduct, compliance, and risk management. Perform other related duties as assigned in support of the Division of Student Success and Dean of Students Office. Other Duties Perform additional duties as assigned by the Associate Vice President for Student Success and Dean of Students to support the broader goals of the Division of Student Success and contribute to the continued success of the university. Additional Information to Applicants This position is designated as essential personnel and may require availability to work evenings, weekends, and during emergency or critical operations as needed. Job Open Date 08/27/2026 Job Close Date (posting closes at midnight) Open Until Filled Yes Normal Work Days Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday Normal Work Hours Start 8:00AM Normal Work Hours End 5:00PM Posting Notices Legal and Required Notices Texas State University is committed to a policy of non-discrimination and equal opportunity for all persons regardless of race, sex, color, religion, national origin or ancestry, age, marital status, disability, veteran status, or any other basis protected by federal or state law in employment, educational programs, and activities and admissions. Employment with Texas State University is contingent upon the outcome of record checks and verifications including, but not limited to, criminal history, driving records, education records, employment verifications, reference checks, and employment eligibility verifications. In accordance with federal law, all new employees must complete the Form I-9 and provide acceptable documentation verifying their identity and authorization to work in the United States. Texas State University participates in E-Verify, and employment is contingent upon the successful verification of work authorization. Employees are required to maintain valid work authorization to satisfy the conditions of Form I9 at all times during their employment and the university makes no implicit or explicit promises to financially support visa or permanent residency applications. Why Choose Texas State University? Join the Texas State Bobcat team and experience a career that offers more than just a job—it’s a path to a brighter future. As a Texas State employee, you’ll enjoy: Exceptional Benefits: Comprehensive health insurance with 100% premium coverage for employees and 50% for dependents, starting on your first day. Generous Time Off: Enjoy vacation, holidays, sick leave, and more to maintain a healthy work-life balance. Wellness and Balance: Access a FREE wellness program, plus mother and family-friendly resources to support your personal well-being. Professional Growth: Explore a wide range of training, development courses, certifications, and educational support programs. Welcoming Campus: Become part of a vibrant Bobcat community with numerous social and professional networks. Tuition Benefits: Take advantage of tuition support for yourself and for your dependent children, making higher education more accessible for your family. Retirement Security: Secure your future with TRS pension, retirement plans, and voluntary savings options with strong employer contributions. At Texas State University, you’re not just an employee—you’re a valued member of a thriving and beautiful campus community. Start your journey with us today. https://www.hr.txst.edu/benefits.html Quick Link https://jobs.hr.txstate.edu/postings/58291 Applicant Document Required Documents Resume Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents DD 214/DD1300 Veteran's Preference Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you possess a bachelor’s degree from an accredited college or university? Yes No * Do you have professional experience and are able to objectively and impartially administer university policies and procedures in the following areas: student conduct, compliance, administrative investigations, risk management, emergency response, expressive activity/free speech response, or a closely related field? Yes No * Do you have experience conducting or supporting administrative investigations, including interviewing involved parties, reviewing and analyzing information or evidence, and preparing written reports or findings? Yes No * Are you willing and able to provide on-site monitoring and response for demonstrations, protests, athletic events, major campus events, and other incidents, including work during evenings, weekends, outdoors, and in large or emotionally charged environments, with or without reasonable accommodation? Yes No * Do you have a master’s degree in higher education, student affairs, criminal justice, or a related field? Yes No * How many years of experience do you have working collaboratively with students, university administrators, law enforcement, emergency management, and other campus partners during sensitive or high-visibility situations? 0 1-2 3-4 5-6 7+ Office of Human Resources 231 N. Guadalupe St. San Marcos, Texas 78666 Phone: 512.245.2557 Fax: 512.245.1942 Email: hr@txstate.edu Monday through Friday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.txstate.edu/postings/57512": {
      "title": "Job Opportunities",
      "description": "Texas State University Applicant Portal | Nontenure Faculty Pool - Department of Engineering Technology Skip to Main Content Job Opportunities at Texas State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Faculty and Academic Resources Human Resources Student Nontenure Faculty Pool - Department of Engineering Technology Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Information Posting Information Position Title Nontenure Faculty Pool - Department of Engineering Technology Job Posting Number 2026143NTL Job Location San Marcos College/Division College of Science and Engineering Department/School Department of Engineering Technology Position Description The Department of Engineering Technology seeks to develop a pool of candidates for possible temporary/nontenured faculty to teach in per course capacity. Temporary/nontenured per course faculty are appointed to teach up to two courses per semester. Teaching appointments are based upon student demand on a semester by semester basis. Eligibility for annual reappointment is contingent upon satisfactory annual performance evaluations during the term period, in addition to continuity of funding and departmental need. Successful candidates may teach at the San Marcos and Round Rock campuses and/or online, depending on the needs of the academic department. Please note that this posting is not intended to convey that such a need currently exists. Applicants applying for a pool faculty position will be available to the departmental search committee for consideration as the need arises. Required Qualifications Specific educational requirements vary by position, but a minimum of a master’s degree with 18 graduate hours in the teaching field is generally required. Preferred Qualifications Previous teaching experience is preferred. Application Procedures The Department of Engineering Technology will accept applications from qualified applicants on a continual basis. Only applications submitted through the Texas State University website will be accepted and considered, https://jobs.hr.txstate.edu/postings/57484 . Interested individuals should submit: A current CV. A letter of interest outlining specific academic and professional experiences relevant for appointment. Unofficial transcripts (all in one document). As part of our application process, we are requesting contact information for three (3) academic references who can provide information about the applicant’s skills and experiences. Please provide the names and email addresses for your academic references in the Reference Letter section of the employment application. Candidates selected for hire will be required to provide official transcripts from all degree granting universities. Type of Position Temporary Full-Time or Part-Time Proposed Start Date Posting Date 06/24/2026 Review Date 08/31/2027 Close Date 05/31/2027 Open Until Filled? No Legal Notices Texas State University is committed to a policy of non-discrimination and equal opportunity for all persons regardless of race, sex, color, religion, national origin or ancestry, age, marital status, disability, veteran status, or any other basis protected by federal or state law in employment, educational programs, and activities and admissions. Employment with Texas State University is contingent upon the outcome of record checks and verifications including, but not limited to, criminal history, driving records, education records, employment verifications, reference checks, drug screening (if applicable), and employment eligibility verifications. Certain positions, including those with clinical responsibilities, require compliance with health, immunization, and other requirements mandated by affiliated clinical agencies. Employment and continued assignment in such roles are contingent upon meeting and maintaining these requirements. This position may conduct research on critical infrastructure depending on the research focus of a hire. Consistent with the requirements of the State of Texas, individuals conducting research on critical infrastructure will be subject to pre-employment, and then subsequent routine background checks/cybersecurity screenings. Employment will at all times be contingent upon the successful completion of these screenings in accordance with State and University requirements. Reasonable Accommodation If you experience accessibility issues or require alternative formatting during the application process due to a disability or underlying condition, we request that you contact Talent Acquisition for assistance. A representative from Talent Acquisition will respond to you as soon as possible. Record Checks and Verifications Employment with Texas State University is contingent upon the outcome of record checks and verifications including, but not limited to, criminal history, driving records, education records, employment verifications, reference checks, and employment eligibility verifications. Eligibility for Employment In accordance with federal law, all new employees must complete the Form I-9 and provide acceptable documentation verifying their identity and authorization to work in the United States. Texas State University participates in E-Verify, and employment is contingent upon the successful verification of work authorization. Employees are required to maintain valid work authorization to satisfy the conditions of Form I9 at all times during their employment and the university makes no implicit or explicit promises to financially support visa or permanent residency applications. Why Work at Texas State? Texas State University is a large, student-centered public research institution serving more than 40,000 students across undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral programs in San Marcos and Round Rock. Classified as a Carnegie Doctoral University with High Research Activity (R2), TXST is actively advancing its ambitious Run to R1 strategy, demonstrating sustained growth in research activity, doctoral education, and scholarly impact. Faculty at TXST join a vibrant academic community of over 5,000 faculty and staff committed to excellence in teaching, research, and service. The university offers a collaborative, supportive environment, and interdisciplinary scholarship. Outstanding faculty are drawn to TXST for the opportunity to make a meaningful impact—advancing research, mentoring a growing student body, and helping shape the future of a rapidly growing research university. Quick Link https://jobs.hr.txstate.edu/postings/57484 Special Instructions Summary Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Unofficial Transcripts (all in one document) Optional Documents DD 214/DD1300 Veteran's Preference Licensure Certifications Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a master's degree in Nutrition with at least 18 graduate credit hours? Yes, I earned a master's degree in Nutrition with at least 18 graduate credit hours. Yes, I anticipate completing a master's degree in Nutrition with at least 18 graduate credit hours by date of hire. No * How many years of teaching experience do you have at the university level? No prior teaching experience. Less than 1 1 year 2 years 3 years 4 years 5 years or more * Describe your prior teaching experience (if any) including experience teaching classes that are fully online or delivered remotely. Include a list of all classes taught. (Open Ended Question) * Which semester(s) do you wish to be considered for? Fall, Spring, Summer or Combination? (Open Ended Question) * What is your schedule availability?  Days/Nights; Days of the Week; Location (e.g., San Marcos, Round Rock, online courses, etc.) (Open Ended Question) Office of Human Resources 231 N. Guadalupe St. San Marcos, Texas 78666 Phone: 512.245.2557 Fax: 512.245.1942 Email: hr@txstate.edu Monday through Friday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
